# Copy to .dev.vars (git-ignored), or let pithy generate one per worktree.
#
# One-time setup:
#   1. Run `wrangler login` — OAuth, used by `wrangler dev` and `wrangler deploy`.
#   2. Set the bootstrap Cloudflare API token below. pithy uses it to call the
#      Cloudflare REST API and to mint scoped, account-owned tokens from there.
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=your-account-id
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-bootstrap-token
#
# Application secrets do NOT live here. `pithy add <capability>` puts them in the dev secrets file,
# outside this repository — run `pithy doctor` to see where. This file is env bindings: the secrets
# master key, public keys like a Turnstile sitekey, and the two credentials above.
#
# The difference is the registry's `backend`. A `d1` secret is read from the secrets store in every
# environment, dev included, so writing one here does nothing — the Worker refuses with "bound as a
# plain value, and a d1 secret is never read from a binding". A `cf-secrets-store` secret has no local
# store to read, so its value does belong here.
